+## 8. Memory Traffic: Write-Allocate vs. No-Write-Allocate
+
+![Memory traffic under both write policies](plots/plot_memory_traffic.svg)
+
+Phase II added two counters to the controller: `mem_reads` and `mem_writes` count completed 32-bit words on the backing-memory interface. Each trace ran from reset at 1 KB, 16 B blocks and $L = 4$ under both policies ([raw CSV](memory_traffic.csv)).
+
+| Trace | Policy | Read Misses | Write Misses | `mem_reads` | `mem_writes` | Total Words |
+|---|---|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|
+| `mixed_rw.trace` | `WA = 0` | 16 | 13 | 64 | 91 | 155 |
+| `mixed_rw.trace` | `WA = 1` | 8 | 8 | 64 | 91 | 155 |
+| `smoke.trace` | `WA = 0` | 5 | 1 | 20 | 2 | 22 |
+| `smoke.trace` | `WA = 1` | 4 | 1 | 20 | 2 | 22 |
+| six read-only traces | either | same | 0 | same | 0 | same |
+
+### Analysis
+1. **Writes are fixed by write-through.** Every CPU write reaches memory under both policies, so `mem_writes` equals the number of CPU writes (91 in `mixed_rw.trace`).
+2. **Reads follow a simple formula.** `mem_reads` = 4 words $\times$ (read misses + `WRITE_ALLOCATE` $\times$ write misses). For `mixed_rw.trace`: $4 \times 16 = 64$ without allocation and $4 \times (8 + 8) = 64$ with it.
+3. **Same traffic, better timing.** In both mixed traces, each block that write allocation fetches on a write miss is read again soon after. Without allocation, that later read misses and fetches the same block. Total traffic is therefore unchanged; allocation only fetches the block earlier. That turns 8 read misses into hits and lowers read AMAT from 4.94 to 3.97 cycles (Section 6).
+4. **When write allocation costs more.** If written data is never read back, allocation adds traffic: an isolated cold write moves 4 read words with allocation and none without. Workloads that write large buffers without reading them back would favor no-write-allocate.
+
+### Takeaway
+For this trace set, write allocation costs no extra memory traffic and improves read latency, because the mixed workload reads what it writes. The result depends on read-after-write reuse, so no-write-allocate remains the safer default for write-streaming workloads.
